---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/firepush-vs-omnisend';
const title = 'Firepush vs Omnisend for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Firepush web push with eligible Omnisend email, SMS and push journeys: permissions, inventory, migration and campaign ownership.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Firepush still send email or SMS?",
    "answer": "Its current vendor notice says Firepush is a web push app and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. The article update date does not establish when those services ended."
  },
  {
    "question": "When can Omnisend fit this campaign?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Omnisend for coordinated email, SMS and web push when the account, plan, destination and sender qualify. Verify identity matching, channel permission, purchase exits and suppression in the configured workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both tools run together?",
    "answer": "Yes, if Firepush owns a defined web push journey and Omnisend owns agreed email or SMS journeys. Assign one operator to cross-channel collisions and choose one owner for web push rather than duplicating it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can browser permission become SMS consent?",
    "answer": "No. Keep browser push permission, email permission and promotional SMS consent separate. A purchase or matched profile alone does not establish permission for another channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will migration preserve every record?",
    "answer": "That is unknown until available exports and destination import rules are inspected. Reconcile consent evidence, suppression, orders and queue state; do not assume browser subscriptions or histories transfer."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs and results be compared?",
    "answer": "Use current account-specific quotes and comparable eligible-customer holdouts. Compare contribution after product, messaging, platform, integration and support costs; do not count discounts or returns twice or treat attributed sales as incremental lift."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Firepush vs Omnisend</nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign ownership comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Firepush vs Omnisend for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Firepush is currently a web push app. Evaluate Omnisend for email and SMS campaign ownership where your account and destinations qualify; keep browser permission separate from those channels.</p>
      </header>
      <TldrBox title="Choose by channel and operating needs" picks={[
        { label: 'Firepush', tool: 'Web push', reason: 'Consider an existing browser audience with a defined journey and measurable contribution.' },
        { label: 'Omnisend', tool: 'Coordinated journeys', reason: 'Evaluate email, SMS and web push with plan, regional and sender eligibility confirmed.' },
        { label: 'Shared control', tool: 'Merchant owner', reason: 'Assign permission checks, inventory truth, suppression and collision handling before launch.' },
      ]} />
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Current product scope</h2>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/targeted-email-marketing/">current Firepush vendor notice</a> says it is a web push app and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. Checked November 25, 2026. November 18, 2026 is the article update date, not an established shutdown effective date. Older app listings and historical tutorials do not override that explicit notice.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.omnisend.com/pricing/">Omnisend lists email, SMS and web push</a>. That makes it a candidate for a team seeking coordinated journeys, subject to current account, plan, destination, sender registration and channel eligibility. Confirm entitlements and charges in your account rather than inferring unlimited access or identical coverage from the channel list.</p>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Firepush</th><th>Omnisend</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Campaign channel</td><td>Current web push role</td><td>Email, SMS and push subject to eligibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing audience</td><td>Inspect browser permission and reachable subscriptions</td><td>Validate identities and permission per channel</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drop coordination</td><td>Test push timing beside other senders</td><td>Test configured segments, exits and cross-channel exclusions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Migration</td><td>Available exports and portability unverified</td><td>Confirm import mappings and rebuild scope</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Merchant scenarios to test</h2>
        <p>These are proposed merchant scenarios, not verified customers or performed experiments. They preserve distinct campaign questions without implying revenue, conversion or vendor performance results.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ember Ceramics: separate maker-story email from a kiln-drop alert; preserve waitlist order and fulfillment capacity before opening access.</li>
          <li>Peak Trail Gear: isolate clearance-sensitive shoppers from full-price loyalists and test whether an incentive adds contribution against a holdout.</li>
          <li>Sauce Lab: use batch inventory as a scarcity gate, with cancellation when stock is stale or exhausted rather than a second automatic alert.</li>
          <li>Ridge Run Coffee: exclude active subscribers from acquisition offers and check subscription-status freshness before queue release.</li>
          <li>Cipher Audio: compare education-led launch timing with an eligible browse-interest alert; test a no-discount offer without claiming a margin gain.</li>
          <li>Patchwork Denim: match style preferences to one-of-one inventory and suppress sold pieces at send time.</li>
          <li>Arena Energy: separate an athlete collaboration announcement from an email-only incentive so the text does not promise an unavailable offer.</li>
          <li>Loom &amp; Frame: disclose edition availability and test an inventory-triggered collector alert against a launch-only treatment.</li>
          <li>Driftwood Surf Co: coordinate seasonal forecast content with a restock waitlist rather than notifying the entire audience.</li>
          <li>Noir Collective: compare restrained drop frequency with the existing calendar while tracking unsubscribes and total contribution per eligible shopper.</li>
          <li>SoleDrop ATX: preserve size-specific priority access and stop general-release messages when the relevant size run is gone.</li>
          <li>Festival Threads Co: map city and venue timezones to tour routing; cancel messages when a date or venue changes.</li>
          <li>Vinyl Vault Records: distinguish pressing waitlists from repeat collectors without treating purchase value as channel permission.</li>
          <li>Bloom &amp; Wick: separate gifting deadlines from self-purchase education and validate regional fulfillment promises.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Identity, permission and purchase exits</h2>
        <p>Browser push permission does not authorize email or SMS. Keep each channel's capture source, purpose, timestamp, disclosure and revocation records. Validate current destination-specific consent and sending restrictions with qualified guidance; a generic quiet-hours window cannot establish compliance everywhere. Apply recipient-local restrictions and test unknown timezone handling before release.</p>
        <p>Use test profiles to check anonymous-to-known matching, shared phone or email conflicts, opt-outs and purchase events. Test a purchase arriving while an alert is queued, delayed order delivery, refunds and duplicate event replay. The acceptance condition is no stale recovery promotion or duplicate send after the configured exit, with an auditable exception queue when identity cannot be resolved.</p>
        <h2>One owner per journey</h2>
        <p>Omnisend can be useful when one team wants to coordinate eligible email and SMS journeys. Verify that its actual configuration supports the needed exclusions and exits. If retaining Firepush for web push, name the push owner and document cross-tool priority, cooldowns and cancellation rules. Do not enable a competing Omnisend push journey for the same audience without a deliberate experiment.</p>
        <p>An existing <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> education sequence may remain for a defined gap with explicit entry rules and exclusions. Adding another email sender is optional; avoid duplicate welcome, winback or offer journeys. Neither product choice removes the merchant's responsibility for inventory and message promises.</p>
        <h2>Migration and reconciliation</h2>
        <p>Inspect available Firepush exports and confirm destination import support before promising portability. Browser subscriptions, consent evidence, suppression history and reporting may have different transfer limits; their availability is unknown here. Never infer fresh permission from imported contact fields.</p>
        <p>Map identifiers, channel status, timestamps, Shopify order IDs and active queue state. Reconcile source and destination counts by channel and suppression reason. Pause overlapping automations during cutover, replay sampled events with deduplication checks, and verify purchase exits before enabling live sends. Assign a cutover owner, rollback conditions and a retained audit trail for unresolved records.</p>
        <h2>Costs and a decision rule</h2>
        <p>Request current quotes for the actual contact mix, destinations, message segments, carrier fees, push volume, integration effort and support. No price or cost ranking is established here. Model normal and peak inventory periods; account for migration labor and duplicate subscriptions while tools coexist.</p>
        <p>Choose a test duration and sample size from the buying cycle and required precision. Use comparable eligible-customer groups and a holdout; reconcile orders across tools to avoid double attribution. Contribution is net sales after discounts and returns, minus product cost, platform and delivery fees, integration and support costs. Deduct each cost once. Compare incremental contribution per eligible shopper, alongside opt-outs, complaints and inventory errors, rather than declaring a winner from attributed revenue per send.</p>
        <p>Retain Firepush when a permissioned web push journey meets the agreed operational and economic criteria. Consider Omnisend when eligible coordinated email and SMS workflows meet those criteria with clear ownership. If neither passes identity, suppression or inventory checks, resolve those gaps before increasing send volume.</p>
        <p>Related operating checks: <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent records</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Firepush vs Omnisend FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
